s day on the hill hoping  to address that stigma  you guys excited  i've never heard you all be excited for  school this father's day hgtv star and  data four brian baumler hopes other dads  will be open and honest with their kids  including about tough stuff i think if  i'd had someone to have those  conversations with you know  25 years ago that would have really  shortened the amount of time that i had  to deal with it dealing with crippling  anxiety that gripped bombler in his 20s  and 30s you'd get the tunnel vision and  the grade out and the the extreme high  heart rate and the sweat  and just the panic the sheer panic of  thinking i'm going to drop dead at any  moment tearing down tough guy  stereotypes bomler is opening up about  his mental health  including at this father's day on the  hill event backed by politicians of all  strikes people are often surprised that  you know that that guy was scared to  leave his house i'm like that guy almost  swallowed an entire bottle of ativan  just to stop  the panic in general stigma around  mental health has been lessening but the  head of the mental health commission of  canada says not so much for men still  less likely to talk about what they're  facing for fear of showing weakness  sadly three-quarters of canadians who  die by suicide are men they may be more  irritable they'd be  consuming more alcohol or cannabis  they may be broody they may be  putting themselves out they mean be not  seeing their friends that often those  are all signs that things could be  not well with them and you need to reach  out it was right after i had my my  second daughter and feeling incredibly  depressed not knowing who i could talk  to who to reach out to at least one in  10 men suffers from depression after the  birth of a child another issue less  talked about  conservative mp matt generous turned his  struggles into advocacy this event now  in its fifth year with a new foundation  called hi dad providing a resource for  men to